Here's the procedural black-and-white breakdown:

First, **use public, open law** — your newly acquired credential from mastering the craft doesn't carry any liabilities if you later find out you did nothing.

Second, **build from scratch first**, ideally with 5A lithium polymer polymer battery** (they're durable, long-lived); never switch off anything or smoke near them. You won't hunt for smoke — they fade horribly.

Third, **priming a circuit board** with a multimeter before soldering matters** — swap polarity until the needle rises 2-4 cm and ends instantly. Voltage trust alone is insufficient; resistance alone matters more.

Fourth, **test audibly** — visually trace each LED to verify proper power and ground connections.

Then verify:

- If it outputs "O," right-zero transmission;
- If it outputs "3-10V," each step down by 10mV increments valid;
- If it displays a multi-number pulse lasting seconds with no apparent pattern, move on;
- If it saves calibration once per year but forgets, wait 24 hours and try again with scanner software;
- If it claims "sloppy" and fades every few months, walk away.

Commercial vendors (Allied Supervisor, Strategic Voting Systems, Mesgar) all document factory tests for documentation purposes. Certification bodies like CSA Silicon Valley don't approve anything that mangles that level of data integrity. Buy a small local enterprise instead — you own the system better, the community owns it.

Finally, **read up on local jurisdiction**. If your state regulates confidentiality, log every second of calibration data intact. Always keep a takedown certificate signed by someone who knows what they're doing.

